rrest  up to 80 percent of the cell population  in a chosen phase of the cell cycle and  perform reliably over multiple  experiments over a time frame of several  weeks  so the way that they used the vibe in  this process was to actually look at the  dna content so in the schematic on the  right hand side of the screen you can  see their process where they are seated  in dishes grown for three days then  they're collecting their cells they're  permalizing them and then they're  staining them for dna content they get  run on the vibe where they gate them for  cells using forward and side scatter  then they look at only their single  cells and then they look at their dna  content and they analyze which phase of  the cell cycle these cells are in  they also had a protocol with transient  infections that you can see is included  in the brackets so if they did the  transfections they actually gated for  their gfp and then looked at the cell  cycle for those gfp positive cells  and so their conclusion was the first  time synthetic rna devices have been  used to control the mammalian cell cycle  these rna platform represents a general  class of synthetic biology tools for  modular dynamic and multi-output control  over mammalian cells  the next group  that's using the vive is focused on  developing a fast economical and high  throughput platform to study chromatin  biology  and so they are studying yeast and so  the way that they use the vibe in their  study  was to process 96 well plates of freshly  induced yeast cultures for antibody  labeling  the vibe was used to detect histones  screen antibodies and for the mapping  and binary crosstalk experiments and you  can see what their conclusion was after  using the vibe for their research it's  on the screen  and then the final example i'm going to  talk today is a group out of singapore  that was looking at p pastorius as a  widely considered industrial workhorse  for recombinant protein production  however genomic integration has a  relatively low transformation efficiency  and some heterogeneity due to random  integration so what they ended up doing  was transfecting yeast with blue  fluorescent protein or bfp expressing  plasmids and then they cultured  then they ran those cells on the vibe  so they analyzed these samples in 96  well plates and they mapped out  blue fluorescent and protein intensities  on heat maps and they did hierarchical  clustering based on the relative  frequencies of fluorescent events in  each gate  so you can see their conclusion here and  how well the vibe worked for what they  were trying to accomplish  here's just a slide about what our  customers are saying  i really like how people refer to the  vibe as something fast rapid and fully  automated  and  how it's very easy to use  so just to summarize it if you had to  listen to this presentation and come  away with four things these are the four  things that i really want you to focus  on the walk away capabilities of the  vibe how you can just load in your  samples import your experiment settings  and leave the staining data acquisition  and the data analysis to the vibe  we can do easy fluorescent protein  analysis because we have  the violet blue and yellow lasers we can  look at fluorescent protein and reporter  applications using mcherry gfp cfp and  many more  we have the flexible assay setup meaning  that we can easily and immediately  switch between our tubes and our plates  we don't have to worry about hooking up  any clunky hts to our system everything  is just going to be placed on the rack  right in front of the instrument and  then the multi-instrument alignment we  can harmonize data with collaborating  labs to ensure the reproducibility of  your assay  thank you sarah for your informative  presentation we will now start the q a  portion of the webinar if you have a  question you'd like to ask please do so  now just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  let's get started  sarah um thank you so much the first  question is  can this equipment fit into a biosafety  cabinet  awesome thanks serena yeah this is a  very compact instrument so it's just  over two feet wide  and then the depth and the height are  just below two feet so it fits perfectly  in a bio safety cabinet and we have  customers who currently have it in a  biosafety cabinet working with microbes  human samples or animal products  fantastic the second question is  can you tell me how the integrated  column works for cell enrichment  yeah so our max quant vibe actually has  an integrated maps column on the system  and so that can be used for automatic  pre-enrichment of rare cell populations  prior to flow cytometry analysis it's  going to follow the same principle as  our traditional max column so briefly  you're going to label your target cells  magnetically with our max micro beads  and then you're going to stain with the  appropriate antibody conjugated  fluorochromes  these label cells are going to be taken  up by the max quant and then passed over  the column the magnetically labeled  cells are retained while the negative  cells pass through the column and then  go directly to the waste container the  magnetically labeled cells are then  washed and eluded and then passed to the  flow cytometer for analysis  fantastic  the third question we have i have a  small sample  i have small sample volumes what is the  minimum sample volume needed for  analysis  yeah so because we have the  the syringe pump driven system uh we can  go as low as 25 microliters that's the  minimum recommended volume for analysis  fantastic  my lab wants to start using a flow  photometer for analysis can you please  tell me more about this training and  science support  yeah so that's one of the the big values  that we offer here at mel tinney so we  have uh hands-on trainings at our  miltoni locations but then we can also  do the real time support with our max  quant live support that's going to  connect you with a phd level scientist  and that's available 24 5 and then we  also have a field application team that  will offer training on site in your lab  and help you develop any flow panels  then if you would like to move forward  with a custom application development we  do have an additional team that's there  to support any application needs that  you may have  very good well thank you again sarah for  your time today and your important  research before we go i'd like to thank  the audience for joining us today and  for their interesting questions  i'd also like to thank our sponsors  miltoni biotech for sponsoring today's  webinar questions we did not have time  for today and those submitted during the  on-demand period will be addressed by  the speaker via the contact information  you provided at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand for  two years till 9 29 23 and labyrinths  will alert you via email when it's  available for replay we encourage you to  share that email with your colleagues  who may have missed today's live event  until next time  goodbye
 

